          'The Party does not want obedience alone; it wants belief in contradictions. Its three slogans stand on the white face of the Ministry of Truth: "War is Peace, Freedom is Slavery, Ignorance is Strength." Doublethink is the mental discipline that makes such statements comfortable, the capacity to hold two opposed ideas at once, accept both, and forget that the trick was ever performed. It is what allows Winston to rewrite a newspaper in the morning and honestly fail to remember the original by the afternoon.\n\nWinston’s private proof that the past is real is a scrap of newspaper he once held in his hands, a photograph placing three condemned men in New York on the very day the Party claimed they were confessing to treason on foreign soil. He drops it into the memory hole anyway. That gesture is the novel in miniature: the evidence exists, an individual recognizes it, and the machinery that swallows it is stronger than the recognition. A regime need not win an argument if it can simply outlast a memory.\n\nThe climax of Winston’s re-education is not about crimes at all but about arithmetic. O’Brien holds up four fingers, demands that Winston see five, and raises the pain until Winston genuinely cannot tell how many are there. Reality exists in the human mind and nowhere else, O’Brien explains, and since the Party controls the only mind that counts, the collective one, it controls reality itself. Truth in Oceania is not discovered; it is issued.',
