        heading: 'Surveillance and the Death of the Private Self',
        body:
          'The telescreen both transmits and receives, and it cannot be switched off, only dimmed. Winston learns to keep his back to it and to arrange his features into an expression of quiet optimism, because the wrong look at the wrong moment is facecrime and carries its own punishment. Orwell’s insight is that surveillance rarely works by catching people; it works by persuading them to supervise themselves. A citizen who assumes he is always watched has already been conquered without a single arrest.\n\nAgainst this, Winston hoards small enclosures of selfhood. There is the alcove where he writes his diary, the glass paperweight with its fragment of coral that he loves precisely because it is useless, and the room above the shop where a heavy prole woman hangs washing and sings beneath the window. Each is a place where he can be a person rather than a function. When the Thought Police finally arrive, a boot smashes the paperweight on the hearthstone, and the tiny world inside it is gone in a second.\n\nSurveillance also colonizes the family. The Parsons children play at hunting traitors and shout accusations in the corridor, and Parsons is eventually denounced by his own small daughter for muttering against Big Brother in his sleep, a betrayal he reports with something close to pride. Where there is no private room there can be no private loyalty either. Orwell abolishes the self in Oceania by abolishing the space in which a self might have formed.',
