        heading: 'The Surveillance Economy of 2026',
        body:
          'Orwell imagined a state that forced a screen into every home. The stranger outcome of the 2020s is that people bought the screens themselves, installed the doorbell cameras, and chose to carry a location beacon in a pocket. Smart speakers listen for a wake word in the kitchen, workplace software counts keystrokes, and facial recognition can pull a face out of a crowded station in seconds. The apparatus Winston fears is now largely voluntary, sold as convenience and safety rather than imposed as control.\n\nThe arguments running through courts and legislatures in 2026 are recognizably Orwell’s arguments. Should police be permitted to run live facial recognition in public space, should predictive systems assign risk scores to people who have done nothing, and who may buy the location data that brokers harvest from ordinary apps? Each question is a version of the one the telescreen poses, which is what exactly separates being protected from being watched.\n\nThe novel’s sharpest warning is not about cameras at all but about behavior. Winston flattens his face into an expression of optimism because he assumes an audience, and researchers now describe a similar chill in people who quietly moderate what they search for, post, and say once they believe a record is being kept. Surveillance changes the watched long before it ever produces an arrest.',
