    summary:
      'Rodion Raskolnikov is a former law student living in a St. Petersburg garret so cramped it is described as more like a cupboard than a room, too proud to take pupils for money and too poor to eat. He has convinced himself that history’s great men simply take what a new idea requires and that conventional morality is a set of rules written for ordinary people. To test whether he belongs to that higher class, and to solve his poverty at a stroke, he takes an axe to Alyona Ivanovna, an old pawnbroker he has decided is a louse feeding on the desperate. The plan collapses within a minute: her gentle half-sister Lizaveta walks in, and he kills her too, then stumbles out with almost nothing and hides the stolen goods under a stone.\n\nWhat follows is not the clarity he expected but weeks of fever, delirium, and a compulsion to walk back toward the scene of his own crime. He is drawn into a long and courteous duel with Porfiry Petrovich, a magistrate who has no evidence and does not particularly need any, since he can keep talking until the suspect breaks himself. Around this runs the ruin of the Marmeladov family, whose daughter Sonya has gone onto the streets to feed her consumptive stepmother’s children, and the danger posed to his sister Dunya by the predatory Svidrigailov. Raskolnikov confesses the murders to Sonya first, in the room where she reads him the raising of Lazarus.\n\nEncircled by his own conscience rather than by proof, he takes Sonya’s advice, goes out to the crossroads, kisses the earth he has defiled, and turns himself in at the police station. He is sentenced to eight years of penal servitude in Siberia, and Sonya follows him there. For most of that exile he stays hard and unrepentant, convinced only that he was weak rather than wrong. The final pages grant him the beginning and not the completion of a change: he weeps at Sonya’s feet, and Dostoevsky closes by promising the story of a gradual renewal that he declines to tell.',
