          'Months before the murder, Raskolnikov publishes an article dividing humanity into two classes. The ordinary must live obediently and produce the next generation; the extraordinary, a Napoleon or a Solon, possess an inner right to step over a corpse, or a river of blood, if a new idea demands it. Porfiry, who has read the piece, questions him about it with maddening politeness, asking how a man is supposed to know which category he belongs to. That question is the whole novel, and Raskolnikov cannot answer it.\n\nDostoevsky refutes the theory not by argument but by watching what it does to the man who holds it. Raskolnikov eventually admits to Sonya that he did not kill to help his mother or to fund a career, but to find out whether he was a louse like everybody else or a man with the right to act. The evidence returns immediately. He faints in a police bureau at the first mention of the crime, hides on a staircase from a caretaker, and lies in bed for days while his body delivers the verdict his mind refuses to accept.\n\nThe target here is larger than one student. Dostoevsky is arguing with the fashionable rational egoism of his day, the utilitarian arithmetic that would trade one worthless life for a hundred useful ones. The novel’s answer is Lizaveta, who was never part of the calculation and who dies anyway with her arm half raised, an ordinary frightened woman standing in for everything a theory cannot account for. Ideas that treat people as units, Dostoevsky insists, eventually meet a person.',
