      'Nick Carraway, a young Midwesterner with a bond-trading job and a small rented house on Long Island, spends the summer of 1922 next door to a mansion where a stranger throws parties every weekend. The stranger is Jay Gatsby, and the rumors circulating about him are wilder than anything anyone can confirm: that he once killed a man, that he was a German spy during the war. Nick eventually learns the plainer and stranger truth. Gatsby was born James Gatz on a North Dakota farm, invented a new name and a new self at seventeen, and assembled a fortune for a single purpose, which is to win back Daisy Buchanan, the woman he loved before the war and who is now married to the brutal, moneyed Tom.\n\nNick arranges the reunion, and for a few weeks Gatsby appears to get what he wants: Daisy in his house, running her hands through his imported shirts and weeping at how beautiful they are. The dream cannot survive contact. In a stifling suite at the Plaza, Tom exposes the bootlegging behind the fortune and forces Daisy to admit that she loved him too, and Gatsby watches the past he has spent five years rebuilding come apart in a single afternoon. Driving back to Long Island in Gatsby’s yellow car, Daisy strikes and kills Myrtle Wilson, Tom’s mistress, and does not stop.\n\nGatsby takes the blame and stands watch all night outside Daisy’s house for a telephone call that never comes. Tom points Myrtle’s grieving husband toward the yellow car, and George Wilson shoots Gatsby in his swimming pool and then himself. Of the hundreds who drank his liquor all summer, almost nobody attends the funeral. Sickened by people who smash things and then retreat into their money, Nick goes back west, and the novel closes on his image of a green light and of boats beaten backward against the current.',
